# Unit tests the one-second budget tolerates, one nextest test id per line.
#
# The budget is 1.000 s of wall clock as observed on the run's own log, held by
# `task rust:test:duration` (scripts/unit_test_duration_gate.py). A test at or
# above it that no line here covers reds the gate.
#
# Numbers below are measured on CI — verify-basic.yml `Smoke (Linux)`,
# ubuntu-latest 4-core — never on a dev box. That runner is roughly 2x slower
# than this project's development machine for the scan-heavy tests and much
# worse for process-spawning ones, and CI is where the gate actually gates. A
# test sitting above ~0.7 s there is a latent red, not a pass; the budget does
# NOT get raised above 1.0 s to buy it headroom. 1.0 s is the contract.
#
# An entry is a DEBT, not a licence. It says "this test is slow and we have not
# fixed it yet", and every line needs a `#` reason above it saying why. The
# list only shrinks: `# TODO(fast-unit-tests):` marks the ones already being
# made fast, so `grep TODO(fast-unit-tests)` is the list of lines to delete.
# Never add one to dodge a red you could fix, and never `#[ignore]` a test
# instead — the skip ceiling catches that, this does not.
#
# A line is an `fnmatch` pattern; one carrying no metacharacter is plain
# equality. A pattern is for a FAMILY that is slow for one structural reason —
# it is one line that cannot rot into a dozen stale ones as runners get faster
# and slower. Everything else stays an exact id, so `grep TODO(fast-unit-tests)`
# remains the list of lines to delete.
#
# An UNBOUNDED pattern is refused outright by the reader, not merely frowned
# upon: a line matching `zzz_no_such_crate zzz_no_such_test` — an id belonging
# to no crate — allows every test in the workspace, and one fat-fingered `*`
# would disable this gate while the file still read as a careful list. Pin the
# crate or a name fragment.
#
# A line that matches nothing at or above the budget is not a failure: the
# `live_powershell_*` tests need `pwsh` on PATH and a machine-dependent red is
# worse than a stale line. The gate prints those as
# `stale allowlist candidate: <line>` instead.
#
# Ids are exactly as nextest prints them after the progress counter:
# `<binary id> <test name>`.

# --- Permanent: real external interpreters -------------------------------
# Each of these spawns a real external interpreter (pwsh, nu, elvish, fish);
# the cost is process start, not test logic, and no amount of test work removes
# it. `.config/nextest.toml` already names this family in the `live-shell`
# test-group and serialises it, because concurrent .NET process starts corrupt
# each other's assembly loading. Anchored on the `live_` naming convention, so
# an ordinary ocx_shell unit test that goes slow is still caught — deliberately,
# and it hides nothing today: of the 316 non-`live_` ocx_shell tests the slowest
# is 0.271 s on this dev box and 0.020 s on CI.
#
# The pattern also spans two module paths (`shell::tests::live_*` and
# `shell::hook::live_shell_tests::*`), which is the second reason it is not an
# enumeration: the CI-only over-budget member (1.607 s, 0.978 s here) lives in
# the one a list written off a local run would never have reached.
#
# On CI this covers 11 tests over budget (37.6 s down to 1.6 s) and three more
# at 0.73-0.84 s that a slower runner will push over.
ocx_shell *live_*

